            %em Hopf invariant one
            refers to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+classes"} homotopy classes
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+functions"} continuous functions
            between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spheres"} spheres
            of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo ⟶
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mi n
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\phi \;\colon\; S^{2n-1} \longrightarrow S^n
                  \\,,
          %p
            hence to elements in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+groups+of+spheres"} homotopy groups of spheres
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ∈
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  [\phi] \in \pi_{2n-1}(S^n)
                  \\,,
          %p
            whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+invariant"} Hopf invariant
            is equal to one:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi h
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  h(\phi) = 1
                  \\,.
          %p
            Often this is regarded not in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integers"} integers
            but in its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quotient"} quotient
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cyclic+group+of+order+2"} cyclic group of order 2
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}/2
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/odd+number"} odd number
          %p
            The
            = succeed "," do
              %em Hopf invariant one problem
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Adams60"} Adams 60
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#RelationToHSpaceStructureAndNormedDivisionAlgebras Relation to H-space structure and normed division algebras
          %p
            The existence of an element of Hopf invariant one in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mi n
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_{2n-1}(S^n)
            is equivalent to the existence of an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/H-space"} H-space
            structure on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^{n-1}
          %p
            A celebrated theorem due to (
            %a{:href => "#Adams60"}> Adams 60
            , introducing and using the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Adams+spectral+sequence"} Adams spectral sequence
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+constructions"} Hopf constructions
            on the four
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normed+division+algebras"} normed division algebras
            (see also at
            = succeed "):" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hurwitz+theorem"} Hurwitz theorem
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+Hopf+fibration"} real Hopf fibration
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+Hopf+fibration"} complex Hopf fibration
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quaternionic+Hopf+fibration"} quaternionic Hopf fibration
            and the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/octonionic+Hopf+fibration"} octonionic Hopf fibration
          %p
            %img{:src => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/AdamsHopfInvariantProofFlow.jpg", :width => "700"}/
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#Adams60"}> Adams 60
            )
          %h2#history History
          %blockquote
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+homotopy+theory"} Stable homotopy theory
              emerged as a distinct branch of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+topology"} algebraic topology
              with
              = succeed "\u2018" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frank+Adams"} Adams
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Adams+spectral+sequence"} his eponymous spectral sequence
              and his spectacular conceptual use of the notion of stable phenomena in his solution to the Hopf invariant one problem.
            %p
              (
              %a{:href => "#ElmendorfKrizMay95"}> Elmendorf-Kriz-May 95
              )
